  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night morning through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 158 PM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nHurricane Isaias Impacts on North and Central GA:\nFrom Friday night through Sunday, Hurricane Isaias will \nmake landfall over the Gulf Coast and then weaken as it moves \nnorthward across Mississippi and Alabama into the TN Valley. North\nand Central Georgia will be on the 'dirty' side of the storm, and\npotentially see significant impacts with a prolonged period of \nstrong winds, heavy rainfall with localized flash flooding, and a \nfew tornadoes.\n\nStrong Winds:\nAs Isaias moves inland Friday night into Saturday, its wind field\nwill expand considerably, with strong winds extending well east of\nthe center of the storm into West-Central GA. A Tropical Storm\nWatch is in effect for this area where wind gusts of 40 to 50 mph\nwill be possible. Further north/east, while Isaias will be\nweakening and becoming post-tropical, there will be a very strong\npressure gradient between it and high pressure over the Mid-\nAtlantic. This gradient will support a prolonged period of strong\neasterly winds with frequent gusts to 40 to 50 mph across most of\nNorth Georgia from early Saturday through early Sunday. A High\nWind Watch has been issued for this area. The prolonged and strong\nwinds could lead to numerous power outages from downed limbs and \ntrees, especially when combined with saturated soils from periods \nof heavy rain. \n\nHeavy Rainfall:\nAs Isaias moves northward through Mississippi/Alabama, it will\nbring widespread rainfall and multiple bands of heavy rain across\nthe area Friday night through Sunday. Rainfall totals of 1 to 3\ninches are likely across much of the area, with localized amounts\nup to 5 inches possible. High rainfall rates (PWAT values of\n2-2.3\") and repeated rounds of heavy rain could lead to localized\nflash flooding, particularly in urban and poor-drainge areas, as\nwell as flood-prone creeks and streams. A Flood Watch has been\nissued for the entire area.\n\nTornadoes:\nAs Isaias tracks northward and inland on Saturday, a favorable\nenvironment for tornadoes will develop across North and Central\nGeorgia, potentially lingering into Sunday morning. Several\ntornadoes will be possible during this period, including the\novernight hours Saturday night. Tornadoes from tropical systems\nare typically quick to develop and short-lived, but with the \nforward speed of the system and the unique setup, we can't rule \nout a stronger (EF2 or EF3), long-track tornado. It is imperative \nthat everyone has a way to receive tornado warnings.\n\nWarm and Dry In the Wake of Isaias:\nOn the backside of Isaias' remnants, a mid- level ridge will build\nover the Southeast US, bringing a warm and dry airmass into the\narea. Rain chances will drop to near zero for Monday- Wednesday,\nwith low temps in the upper 50s to low 60s, and high temps in the\nlow to mid 80s.",
